# Latency RATCHET corpus — the deterministic drive behind `just latency-gate`.
#
# Same JSONL schema and same harness as keystone.jsonl (see that file's header
# and docs/Testing/HandAuthoredRegressions.md); this sidecar is separate only
# because its purpose is different. keystone.jsonl pins CORRECTNESS repros; this
# file pins a fixed WORKLOAD whose only job is to emit enough `stage=e2e` and
# `stage=action_total` samples per rung for a stable statistic.
#
# WHY DETERMINISTIC REPLAY RATHER THAN THE RANDOM KEYSTONE
# A ratchet compares a statistic against a fixed ceiling, so the sample
# population must not move between runs. Random proptest draws give each rung a
# different (and sometimes zero) sample count per run, which would make the gate
# flap and would let a rung silently vanish from coverage. Replaying a fixed
# transition list gives every run the same rungs and the same counts — and the
# gate's --min-samples floor turns any drift into a hard failure instead of a
# vacuous pass. This also follows the standing directive that deterministic
# keystone replay lives in a hand-rolled JSONL, never in a proptest seed.
#
# WHY THE REPETITION COUNTS ARE >= 20 (this is a statistic fix, not padding)
# `measure_latency.py` derives percentiles by linear interpolation, so at small
# n the p95 is mostly the largest sample: at n=12 it is 0.55*v[10] + 0.45*v[11],
# giving the MAX ~45% of the weight. A single slow sample from machine
# contention then moves p95 by ~1.9x, which is exactly what made an earlier
# p95-only version of this gate exit 0/1/0 on three back-to-back runs of an
# unmodified tree. At n=21 the p95 interpolates to exactly v[19] — the
# SECOND-largest sample — and the max carries no weight at all. Each rung below
# is therefore driven >= 20 times. Do not reduce these counts to make the gate
# faster; that silently re-arms the flap.
